// Concept texts, batch 10 (names SECONDARY TRAUMATIC STRESS to THANATOMANIAC).
// Row: [name, group code, short, feels, looks, misreading, help]. An empty help text uses the group's default.
// Group codes: P phobia, A anxiety, M mood, D disorder, T thinking pattern, C control or manipulation tactic,
// R trait or relationship pattern, V harmful habit, U strength or virtue, K practice or tool,
// F fascination or urge, B body or medical.
export default [
  ["SECONDARY TRAUMATIC STRESS", "D", "Stress that builds from hearing about or witnessing other people's trauma, often in helping jobs.", "Vivid stories or images can return during quiet moments, and the body may feel on guard. Sleep, mood and patience can all feel the strain.", "A person may seem tense, withdrawn, or quick to anger, or may avoid talking about their work.", "It is not a sign of weakness or poor empathy. It is a normal response to repeated contact with painful stories, and it can ease with rest and support.", ""],
  ["SECRETIVE", "R", "Tending to keep information, feelings or plans hidden from others, even when there is no need.", "Keeping things private can feel safe and in control. It can also bring loneliness, worry about being found out, and strain in close relationships.", "Others may notice vague answers, closed doors, or a habit of avoiding questions about daily life.", "Privacy is a healthy right. Secrecy becomes a problem when it hides harm or pushes away people who care.", ""],
  ["SECURE ATTACHMENT FORMATION", "U", "Learning, often in childhood, that caring people are reliable and that closeness feels safe.", "Closeness can feel calm and steady, with trust that others will return. Reaching out feels less risky, and disagreements feel more repairable.", "A person may comfortably ask for help, share feelings, and recover after conflict.", "Attachment can be built at any age through steady, caring relationships. Past experiences shape it, but they do not fix it for life.", ""],
  ["SEDUCTIVE NARCISSIST", "R", "A person who charms others to win admiration, then withdraws warmth once they gain nothing.", "Early on, the attention can feel intense and flattering. Later, the sudden coldness can leave a person confused, anxious and trying to earn back approval.", "Others may notice warmth at first, then sudden distance and a constant need to be seen as special.", "Charm is not proof of love or of a fixed character. The pattern is about how someone uses attention, and it can be recognized over time.", ""],
  ["SELECTIVE AMNESIA", "D", "Losing memory of certain painful events or personal details, often after overwhelming stress or trauma.", "A gap can feel confusing and frightening, as if part of life is missing. Memories may return slowly, in pieces, or not at all.", "Others may notice missing details about the past, sudden changes of subject, or distress when a topic comes up.", "Forgetting a painful event is not a choice or a lie. It is a protective response that a doctor or trauma therapist can help explore.", ""],
  ["SELECTIVE MUTISM", "D", "A condition where a person who can speak stays silent in certain settings, such as school.", "Speaking can feel blocked, even when the words are clear in the mind. Fear and pressure can grow in certain places, and silence can feel like the only safe choice.", "A child may talk freely at home but say nothing at school, or may use gestures to communicate in public.", "It is not stubbornness or rudeness. It is an anxiety-related condition that often eases with early speech therapy and mental health support.", ""],
  ["SELECTIVE VALIDATION", "C", "Praising or affirming someone only when it serves the speaker's goal, and withholding approval otherwise.", "Approval arrives unpredictably, so the person keeps trying to earn it. Self-doubt grows, and the search for praise can take over.", "A person may praise one choice and ignore another, or give warmth only after compliance.", "Occasional praise is normal. This tactic matters when approval is used to steer someone away from their own judgment.", ""],
  ["SELENOPHILE", "F", "A love of the moon and its light, phases and quiet presence at night.", "Watching the moon can feel calm and wondrous, and it can bring a sense of connection to something larger. Night walks may feel peaceful.", "A person may stay outside to watch the sky, keep moon journals, or talk often about the moon's phases.", "Loving the moon is healthy and common. It is simply a gentle interest, not a sign of anything wrong.", ""],
  ["SELF-AGGRANDIZEMENT", "R", "Inflating your own importance or achievements to gain admiration, or to feel safer from criticism.", "Being seen as special can bring a brief lift, but it may also bring pressure to keep up the image. Doubts may lurk under the surface.", "Others may notice constant talk about one's own success, quick dismissal of others' work, and discomfort with praise for anyone else.", "Wanting recognition is human. The pattern becomes a problem when it pushes out honest self-reflection and respect for others.", ""],
  ["SELF-BETRAYAL", "V", "A repeated pattern of acting against your own values, needs or promises to yourself.", "It can bring guilt, a sense of being unreliable, and a quiet loss of self-respect. Each broken promise can make the next one harder to keep.", "A person may say yes when they mean no, skip their own rest, or drop goals they once cared about.", "It is not proof that you lack character. Patterns like this often come from fear, habit, or old pressure, and they can change with small, steady steps.", ""],
  ["SELF-BLAME", "T", "A habit of holding yourself fully responsible for problems, even when other factors played a part.", "Guilt and shame can feel constant, and the mind may replay mistakes again and again. Relief can feel out of reach.", "A person may apologize often, take on blame for others, or say they are the problem in every conflict.", "Taking responsibility for your part is healthy. Blaming yourself for everything is not accurate, and it often adds pain without fixing anything.", ""],
  ["SELF-COMPASSION BREAK", "K", "A short pause to name your pain, remember that others struggle too, and offer yourself kindness.", "A break like this can soften harsh self-talk and bring a sense of warmth. It may feel awkward at first, and that is normal.", "A person may pause quietly, place a hand on the chest, or take a slow breath before continuing.", "It is not self-pity or giving up on goals. It is a brief way to meet hard feelings with care, so you can then act more wisely.", ""],
  ["SELF-COMPASSION FLOW", "K", "A gentle, ongoing way of treating yourself with kindness as you move through daily tasks.", "Kindness can feel steady and natural, like a current beneath the day. Mistakes may still sting, but they can pass more quickly.", "A person may speak to themselves with patience, pause before judging, and give themselves room to rest.", "Flow does not mean you never feel hurt. It means practicing kindness during ordinary moments too, not only during crises.", ""],
  ["SELF-COMPASSION PRACTICE", "K", "A regular habit of meeting your own suffering with understanding, warmth and patience.", "Regular practice can gradually soften harsh self-talk. Early on, kind words may feel forced, but comfort often grows with repetition.", "A person may journal kind notes to themselves, use a soothing phrase, or name what they need.", "It is not indulgence or lowering your standards. It helps you grow from mistakes rather than being crushed by them.", ""],
  ["SELF-DEFEATING", "T", "Thoughts or actions that quietly undermine your own goals, often without you noticing at first.", "Self-defeating thoughts can feel like realistic warnings. They can bring discouragement and a sense that effort will not matter.", "A person may say they will try, then avoid the task, or describe every plan as doomed before starting.", "A harsh inner critic is not the same as honest judgment. Noticing the pattern lets you test those thoughts and choose a smaller next step.", ""],
  ["SELF-DESTRUCTIVE BEHAVIORS", "V", "Actions that harm your health, safety or relationships, often to ease pain or escape feelings.", "These actions can bring brief relief, followed by shame, fear, or a deeper sense of hopelessness. The urge may feel hard to resist.", "Others may notice risky choices, unexplained injuries, sudden withdrawal, or giving away belongings.", "Wanting relief is human, and these actions are not a character flaw. Support can ease the pain, and no one has to face it alone.", "If you have thoughts of harming yourself, call or text 988 in the US now, and call 911 if you are in danger."],
  ["SELF-EXPRESSION", "U", "Sharing your thoughts, feelings and creative ideas in your own honest words.", "Expressing yourself can feel freeing and alive. It may also feel exposed, especially when others might not understand.", "A person may speak openly, make art or music, or share personal stories with trusted friends.", "Self-expression is not the same as saying everything to everyone. Choosing when and with whom to share is also healthy.", ""],
  ["SELF-FORGIVENESS JOURNEY", "K", "A gradual process of letting go of self-blame for past mistakes, one honest step at a time.", "Forgiving yourself can bring relief, but it may come in waves. Some days the old guilt returns, and that does not mean you are failing.", "A person may name a mistake, make amends where possible, and then choose to stop punishing themselves for it.", "Self-forgiveness does not erase harm or mean forgetting it. It means accepting responsibility and then allowing yourself to move forward.", ""],
  ["SELF-GASLIGHTING", "T", "Doubting your own memory, perception and feelings until you no longer trust them.", "You may feel foggy, anxious and unsure what is real. Decisions can stall because you do not trust your own judgment.", "A person may say 'I must be wrong', ask others to confirm everything, or apologize for events they remember clearly.", "Doubt can be healthy, but constant self-doubt is different. Writing down facts and checking them with a trusted person can help you rebuild trust in your own mind.", ""],
  ["SELF-HONORING", "U", "Treating your needs, values and limits with respect and making choices that protect them.", "Honoring yourself can feel steady and grounded. It can bring pride and a quiet relief when you keep a promise to yourself.", "A person may take rest seriously, say no to what drains them, and keep commitments to their own growth.", "It is not selfishness. It means treating yourself as someone who matters, which often makes you more able to care for others.", ""],
  ["SELF-ISOLATION", "V", "Withdrawing from friends, family and activities for long stretches, often when feeling low or anxious.", "Being alone can feel safer, yet it can also bring loneliness and a sense of falling behind. Reaching out can feel too hard.", "Others may notice missed plans, unanswered messages, or a person who stays home and stops joining in.", "Needing quiet time is healthy. Isolation becomes a concern when it lasts, deepens low mood, or cuts you off from support.", ""],
  ["SELF-LOATHING", "M", "An intense hatred or disgust toward yourself, often tied to shame and harsh inner criticism.", "Self-loathing can feel like a constant weight and a belief that you are unworthy of kindness. It can make it hard to accept care from others.", "A person may put themselves down often, dismiss compliments, or treat their needs as unimportant.", "These feelings are painful, but they are not an accurate measure of your worth. They often ease with support, and therapy can help you find a fairer view of yourself.", ""],
  ["SELF-SABOTAGE", "V", "Acting in ways that undermine your own goals, relationships or progress, often without intending to.", "It can feel like a tug of war between what you want and what you do. Afterward, frustration and self-criticism often follow.", "A person may put off important tasks, drop plans just before they succeed, or pick fights when things go well.", "Self-sabotage often grows from fear of failure or of change, not from laziness. Naming the fear can make it easier to choose a different next step.", ""],
  ["SELF-SABOTEUR", "R", "A person who repeatedly undermines their own success, often in ways that are hard to see.", "The person may feel stuck, ashamed and unsure why the same setbacks keep happening. Others close to them may feel frustrated and confused.", "Others may notice missed chances, last-minute withdrawal, or stopping just short of a goal.", "The label describes a pattern, not a fixed identity. Patterns like this can change with awareness, practice and support.", ""],
  ["SELF-SOOTHING", "K", "Calming yourself through gentle sensory comfort, such as a warm drink, soft music or slow breathing.", "Soothing can bring a sense of safety and ease in the body. It can take a few minutes before the calm arrives.", "A person may wrap themselves in a blanket, step outside for fresh air, or hum a familiar song.", "Self-soothing is a healthy skill. It becomes a problem only when it replaces dealing with the real issue every time.", ""],
  ["SELF-TRUST REBUILDING", "K", "A practice of slowly restoring your trust in your own judgment after letdowns or betrayal.", "Trust returns in small moments, such as keeping a promise to yourself. Old doubts may still appear, and that is part of the process.", "A person may write down decisions, follow through on small commitments, and notice what they learned.", "Self-trust is built through many small acts, not one big decision. It does not require being certain about everything.", ""],
  ["SENSES ANCHOR", "K", "A grounding practice that uses the senses to bring attention back to the present moment.", "Noticing sights, sounds and textures can loosen the grip of worry. It can bring a steadier feeling within a few minutes.", "A person may pause and quietly name five things they can see, two they can hear, and one they can touch.", "It is not a test or a trick to make feelings disappear. It is a simple way to reconnect with the present, and it can be repeated as often as needed.", ""],
  ["SENSORY OVERLOAD", "A", "Too much input from sound, light, touch or crowds, which can leave the mind and body overwhelmed.", "It can feel like being flooded, with a rush of tension, irritability or a need to escape. Thinking may become harder.", "A person may cover their ears, leave a noisy room, go quiet, or seem suddenly irritable in busy places.", "Sensitivity to input is common, especially with stress, illness, or some developmental differences. It is not a weakness, and planning quiet breaks can help.", ""],
  ["SENSORY RESOURCING", "K", "Using comforting sensations, such as a textured object or warm water, to feel steadier.", "A familiar texture or temperature can bring a sense of safety and presence. The effect can build with regular use.", "A person may hold a smooth stone, wrap their hands in a warm cloth, or touch a favorite fabric during stress.", "It is not a cure, and it does not replace treatment. It is one tool among others that can help you feel more grounded.", ""],
  ["SERENE", "M", "A calm, peaceful state in which the mind feels clear, unhurried and at rest.", "Serenity can feel soft and spacious, with less pull from worry. The body may relax and the breath slow down.", "A person may speak slowly, move without rush, and seem at ease even when things are busy.", "Serenity is not the absence of difficulty or a sign of not caring. It is a steadier state that can sit beside hard feelings.", ""],
  ["SERENE CLARITY", "U", "A clear, calm way of seeing a situation without the fog of stress or rushed judgment.", "Clarity can feel light and focused, as if the mind has more room. Decisions may feel simpler when the noise quiets.", "A person may speak plainly, sort priorities quickly, and stay steady when others feel rushed.", "Clarity does not mean knowing everything. It means seeing more clearly what you know and what you still need to learn.", ""],
  ["SERENE EMOTIONAL FREEDOM", "U", "A sense of being free to feel emotions fully without being ruled by them.", "Feelings can come and go with more ease, without a sense of being trapped. There is room to notice what you feel and choose what to do.", "A person may name feelings openly, let them pass, and respond with thought rather than reflex.", "Emotional freedom does not mean never feeling strong emotions. It means not being controlled by them, which takes practice.", ""],
  ["SERENE EMOTIONAL MASTERY", "U", "Skill in understanding and steering your emotions with calm and care.", "Mastery can feel steady and confident, since you know how to return to balance. Strong feelings may still arise, but they feel less overwhelming.", "A person may pause before reacting, label feelings accurately, and choose a helpful response.", "Mastery is not suppressing feelings or never having setbacks. It grows from practice and self-kindness, not from perfection.", ""],
  ["SERENE INNER PEACE", "U", "A quiet sense of peace within yourself that remains even when life feels busy.", "Inner peace can feel warm and grounded, like a steady center. Thoughts still come, but they pass without pulling you far.", "A person may seem unhurried, respond with patience, and rest comfortably in quiet moments.", "Inner peace is not constant happiness or the lack of problems. It is a steadier place to meet problems from.", ""],
  ["SERENE SELF-COMPASSION", "U", "A calm, steady kindness toward yourself, especially when you are struggling or have made mistakes.", "Gentle self-kindness can feel like a warm hand on the shoulder. It can soften the sting of mistakes and make it easier to try again.", "A person may speak to themselves without harshness and respond to setbacks with patience.", "Self-compassion is not making excuses. It keeps you connected to your values while you take responsibility with care.", ""],
  ["SEXUAL MANIPULATOR", "R", "A person who uses pressure, guilt or deceit to get sexual compliance from a partner.", "The partner may feel pressured, confused and unable to say no without fear of conflict. Trust in their own boundaries can wear down.", "Others may notice repeated pressure after a no, guilt trips, or comments that make a partner feel they owe sex.", "A no is always enough, and no one owes sex to anyone. Coercion is a serious harm, not a misunderstanding or a normal part of relationships.", "The National Domestic Violence Hotline is 1-800-799-7233 in the US. A trusted person, counselor, or advocate can help plan next steps. In the US, call or text 988 if you are in crisis, or call 911 if someone is in danger."],
  ["SHADOW PROJECTION", "T", "Seeing in other people the traits you dislike in yourself, and reacting to them strongly.", "Strong irritation or judgment toward someone can feel justified in the moment. Later, it may feel surprisingly personal.", "A person may criticize others harshly for faults they deny in themselves, or react with unusual anger toward a trait.", "Disliking a trait in others is not proof that you hold it. Projection is a common habit of mind, and it can be noticed and loosened with honest reflection.", ""],
  ["SHADOW WORK INTEGRATION", "K", "A practice of noticing, accepting and owning the hidden or rejected parts of yourself.", "Facing hidden parts can bring discomfort, then a sense of wholeness. Some insights may sting, and others may bring surprising relief.", "A person may journal about traits they judge, ask trusted friends for honest feedback, and speak about their faults with curiosity.", "Shadow work is not proof that you are secretly bad. It is a way to understand yourself fully and reduce the power of hidden reactions.", ""],
  ["SHADOWPUPPETOPHILE", "F", "A deep fondness for shadow puppets and the stories told through light and silhouettes.", "Watching shapes move against a light can feel magical and calming. Some people feel drawn to the quiet craft behind it.", "A person may collect puppets, attend shadow theater, or make small shadow scenes at home.", "This is a harmless creative interest, and many people enjoy it. Shadow puppetry has a long history in many cultures around the world.", ""],
  ["SHAKING IT OFF", "K", "A simple practice of gently shaking the hands, arms and legs to release built-up tension.", "Shaking can bring a loosened, lighter feeling after a stressful moment. The body may tremble a little, and that is a natural release.", "A person may shake out their hands after a tense meeting or bounce gently on their feet before speaking.", "It does not erase the stress or the cause of it. It is a short body-based reset that can make the next step easier.", ""],
  ["SHALLOW BREATHING", "B", "Quick, small breaths that stay high in the chest, often during stress or fear.", "Breathing this way can make the chest feel tight and the head feel light. It can add to the sense of alarm.", "A person may sigh often, speak in short bursts, or seem to struggle to catch their breath.", "Shallow breathing is often a stress response, but ongoing or severe breathlessness needs medical checking.", ""],
  ["SHAME", "M", "A painful feeling that you are bad or unworthy, rather than that you did something wrong.", "Shame can feel hot in the face, heavy in the chest, and urgent to hide. It can pull a person away from others.", "A person may avoid eye contact, apologize repeatedly, or shrink from praise.", "Guilt says 'I did something wrong'. Shame says 'I am wrong', and that second belief is not an accurate picture of who you are.", ""],
  ["SHAME SPIRAL", "M", "A cycle in which shame leads to hiding, and hiding deepens the shame.", "Each turn can feel heavier, with more self-criticism and a sense of being stuck. Relief seems to come only from pulling away.", "A person may withdraw after a mistake, stop answering messages, or repeat apologies for a long time.", "The spiral is a pattern, not a verdict on your worth. Breaking it often starts with one small act of reaching out.", ""],
  ["SHAMING", "C", "Making someone feel small, bad or embarrassed about who they are, in order to control them.", "The person on the receiving end can feel humiliated, silenced and afraid to ask for things. Self-worth can erode when the criticism is constant.", "Others may notice mocking remarks about someone's background, family or habits, or public put-downs.", "Feedback about a choice is different from shaming a person. The pattern matters when the goal is to make someone feel worthless.", "The National Domestic Violence Hotline is 1-800-799-7233 in the US. A trusted person, counselor, or advocate can help plan next steps. In the US, call or text 988 if you are in crisis, or call 911 if someone is in danger."],
  ["SHIVERING", "B", "Rapid, involuntary trembling that can warm the body or follow fear, cold or stress.", "Shivering can feel chilly, shaky and out of control. It often passes once the body warms or the stress eases.", "A person may tremble, rub their arms, or have chattering teeth.", "Shivering is a normal body response, and it is not a sign of weakness. Shaking that comes with fever, confusion or chest pain needs urgent medical care.", ""],
  ["SHUTDOWN", "M", "A state of emotional numbness, in which a person stops responding or feels unable to act.", "Shutdown can feel like a fog, with distant thoughts and a heavy body. It can come on quickly under overwhelm.", "Others may notice blank expressions, short replies, or someone going silent in the middle of a conversation.", "It is not laziness or not caring. It is often the nervous system's way of protecting itself when things feel like too much.", ""],
  ["SHUTDOWN RESPONSE", "B", "An automatic stress reaction in which the body goes still, numb or limp when threat feels overwhelming.", "The body can feel heavy, far away or strangely calm, even when the mind is alarmed. It can be frightening afterward.", "A person may freeze, go limp, or seem unresponsive for a short time.", "It is a survival reflex, not a choice or a moral failure. It can be understood and gently worked with in therapy.", ""],
  ["SICKLE CELL DISEASE", "B", "An inherited blood disorder that makes red blood cells misshapen, causing pain and low blood counts.", "Pain episodes can come on suddenly and last for hours or days, and they can be exhausting. Living with it can bring worry about future flare-ups.", "Others may notice a person pausing to rest during pain, missing work or school, or seeming worn out.", "It is not caused by poor care or a lack of effort. Good treatment, pain plans and regular checkups can improve daily life for many people.", ""],
  ["SILENT TREATMENT", "C", "Refusing to speak to someone for long periods to punish, control or hurt them.", "The person on the receiving end may feel anxious, rejected and desperate to fix things. Walking on eggshells can become a daily habit.", "Others may notice ignored messages, cold silence after a disagreement, or a pattern of withdrawing as punishment.", "Needing space after a fight can be healthy when you say so and return. Silence used as punishment is different, and it harms trust.", "The National Domestic Violence Hotline is 1-800-799-7233 in the US. A trusted person, counselor, or advocate can help plan next steps. In the US, call or text 988 if you are in crisis, or call 911 if someone is in danger."],
  ["SINNER", "T", "A belief that you are morally wrong at the core, beyond forgiveness, because of past mistakes.", "Guilt can feel endless, and forgiveness can seem out of reach. The belief may lead to hiding, fear of judgment, or giving up on change.", "A person may apologize constantly, avoid spiritual or community life, or say they do not deserve kindness.", "Making mistakes does not make a person beyond repair. Many faith and ethical traditions teach repair, forgiveness and a fresh start.", ""],
  ["SITOMANIAC", "F", "A strong, repeated craving or fixation on food, which can take up much of the day.", "Thoughts about food can feel constant and hard to ignore, bringing hunger, guilt or frustration. Eating may bring relief for a short time.", "Others may notice frequent talk about food, sneaking snacks, or eating far more than usual in one sitting.", "Strong hunger can come from illness, medicines, stress or an eating disorder, so it deserves a medical look rather than blame. It is not a moral failing or a lack of willpower.", "A doctor or eating-disorder specialist can help. In the US, call or text 988 any time you are struggling."],
  ["SITUATIONAL AVOIDANCE", "A", "Steering clear of certain places or situations because they bring fear or discomfort.", "Avoiding brings quick relief, which makes it easier to avoid next time. Over months, life can shrink.", "A person may decline invitations, take long detours, or leave early from certain places.", "Avoidance is a normal first reaction, but it can keep fear strong over time. Facing the situation in small, supported steps often helps people regain choices.", ""],
  ["SITUATIONAL PHOBIA", "P", "A strong fear that appears only in specific situations, such as flying, elevators or crowds.", "The fear can rise quickly, with a racing heart, shallow breath or a strong urge to escape. Afterward, the person may feel embarrassed.", "A person may avoid certain places, ask for a different seat, or leave a situation abruptly.", "Phobias are real fears, not weakness or silliness. They often respond well to structured, step-by-step treatment.", ""],
  ["SLEEP DISTURBANCES", "B", "Repeated trouble falling asleep, staying asleep or getting restful sleep most nights.", "Tired days can bring irritability, poor focus and a worry about the next night. Sleep can feel like a goal that keeps slipping away.", "Others may notice frequent yawning, irritability, or missed plans after poor nights.", "Sleep problems are common and often have treatable causes, such as stress, pain, medicines, or breathing problems during sleep. A doctor can help find the cause.", ""],
  ["SLEEP ONSET", "B", "The time it takes to move from wakefulness into sleep, which can run long or uneven.", "Lying in bed can feel frustrating while the mind stays busy. Watching the clock can feel loud, and worry can build with each passing minute.", "A person may get up often, check the time, or lie awake long after getting into bed.", "Taking a while to fall asleep is common, especially under stress. Getting out of bed for a quiet activity can often help the mind settle.", ""],
  ["SLOTH", "V", "An old term for avoiding effort or activity, often linked to low energy, low mood or boredom.", "Avoiding tasks can bring short relief, followed by guilt and a heavier to-do list. Starting can feel harder each day.", "A person may put off chores, sleep late, or leave responsibilities for others.", "Low energy often has a cause, such as depression, illness or poor sleep. It is rarely just a lack of will, and harsh self-blame makes it harder to change.", ""],
  ["SLOWED DIGESTION (PERISTALSIS CHANGES)", "B", "Slower movement of food through the gut, which can cause bloating, discomfort or constipation.", "Peristalsis is the rhythmic muscle movement that pushes food along the gut. When it slows, the stomach can feel full, heavy or crampy.", "A person may say they feel bloated after meals, go to the toilet less often, or feel uncomfortable for long periods.", "Slowed digestion is often linked to stress, medicines, low activity, or illness, and it is not always serious. Ongoing pain, vomiting, or blood in the stool needs a doctor's review.", ""],
  ["SMEAR CAMPAIGN", "C", "A coordinated effort to damage someone's reputation with false or misleading claims.", "The person being targeted may feel humiliated, isolated and unsure who to trust. Being labeled unfairly can be exhausting and frightening.", "Others may notice rumors spreading fast, edited screenshots, or the same accusations repeated across several groups.", "Criticism can be fair, but a smear campaign uses falsehoods or distortions to harm. Keeping dated records and speaking with a trusted advisor can help.", ""],
  ["SMEAR CAMPAIGNER", "R", "A person who repeatedly spreads false or damaging claims to ruin another person's reputation.", "Someone on the receiving end may feel anxious, betrayed and watched by others. Trust in friends and colleagues can fall apart.", "Others may notice repeated hostile posts, private messages passed around, or pressure on others to take sides.", "Disagreement is not a smear campaign. The pattern matters when claims are knowingly false or distorted to cause harm, and it can often be documented.", ""],
  ["SMEARER", "R", "A person who habitually attacks others' character with insults, rumors or half-truths.", "Being attacked this way can feel demoralizing and unfair, especially when the attacks come from someone close. Doubt can spread quickly.", "A person may make cutting remarks in group chats, pass along gossip as fact, or belittle others in public.", "Venting or criticizing sometimes happens to everyone. A smearer repeats attacks to lower someone's standing, and the pattern is worth noticing early.", ""],
  ["SNEEZE REFLEX", "B", "A sudden, automatic sneeze that clears the nose of dust, pollen or irritation.", "A sneeze can come on fast, bringing a brief pressure in the head and a rush of air. It usually passes quickly.", "A person may sneeze several times in a row, cover their nose, or rub their eyes.", "Sneezing is a normal protective reflex. Frequent sneezing with fever, breathing trouble or long symptoms should be checked by a doctor.", ""],
  ["SNUFFOPHILE", "F", "A fascination with real death or killing, sometimes linked to sexual arousal from violence.", "The pull can feel secret, confusing and shameful, especially when it brings up sexual feelings. Fantasies about harm can feel intense and hard to shake.", "Usually private, with few outward signs, and it may show only in private searches or hidden content.", "Having a disturbing interest is not the same as acting on it, and talking about it is a healthy first step. Acting on violent urges would seriously harm real people.", "A therapist who works with sexual health can help you talk it through. In the US, call or text 988 if you are in crisis. If someone is in danger, call 911 now."],
  ["SOCIAL CHAMELEON", "R", "A person who shifts opinions and behavior to fit whoever they are with, often to avoid conflict.", "Blending in can bring safety and approval in the moment. Over time, the person may feel unsure who they really are.", "Others may notice quick changes of view, agreeing with opposite sides, or a different personality in each group.", "Adapting your tone to a setting is a skill. Changing your core values to please people is a pattern that can cost trust and self-respect.", ""],
  ["SOCIAL ENGINEERING", "C", "Tricking people into sharing private information or taking action by posing as someone they trust.", "The person being tricked may feel embarrassed or rushed after realizing it. Pressure to act quickly is often part of the tactic.", "Others may notice urgent messages, unexpected requests for passwords or codes, or pressure to keep a request secret.", "Anyone can be fooled, especially when rushed, and it is not a sign of foolishness. Pause and check requests through a known number, and never share codes or passwords under pressure.", ""],
  ["SOCIAL WITHDRAWAL / ISOLATION", "V", "A pattern of pulling back from people and activities, often during low mood or anxiety.", "Being alone can feel safer, but it can also bring loneliness and a sense of falling behind. Each canceled plan can make the next one harder.", "Others may notice missed events, slower replies, or a person who stops joining regular activities.", "Needing quiet is normal. It becomes a concern when it lasts for weeks or steadily shrinks your support.", ""],
  ["SOLITUDOPHILE", "F", "A love of solitude and quiet time alone, for rest, reflection or creative focus.", "Time alone can feel restorative, with room to think and breathe. Going back into the world may feel a little slower at first.", "A person may choose quiet hobbies, decline some invitations, or enjoy long walks alone.", "Liking solitude is healthy and common. It differs from isolation, which is pulling away from support when you need it.", ""],
  ["SOMATIC", "B", "Relating to the body, such as physical sensations or symptoms that are linked to feelings.", "Strong emotions can show up in the body as tightness, heat, or a sinking stomach. These signals are real, even when their cause is unclear.", "A person may mention aches, tension or stomach upset during stressful times.", "Somatic means connected to the body, not imagined. Body signals are worth attention, and a doctor should check any new or severe symptoms.", ""],
  ["SOMATIC ANXIETY", "A", "Worry that shows up mainly in the body, through a racing heart, tight chest or stomach knots.", "The body may feel on high alert even when nothing dangerous is happening. Physical sensations can make worry feel more urgent.", "A person may fidget, sweat, sigh often, or say their stomach hurts before stressful events.", "Body symptoms of anxiety are real, though they can feel like a medical emergency. Checking with a doctor can rule out physical causes and calm the fear.", ""],
  ["SOMATIC DISSOCIATION", "D", "Feeling cut off from body sensations, so the body may seem numb, distant or unreal.", "Dissociation is the mind's way of creating distance from overwhelming experience. It can feel like the body belongs to someone else.", "A person may seem unaware of pain, cold or hunger, or move in a stiff or robotic way.", "It is a protective response that often follows trauma or intense stress, and it is not a sign of weakness. Support from a therapist who understands trauma can help the body feel safe again.", ""],
  ["SOMATIC FLASHBACKS", "D", "Sudden physical sensations, such as pain, heat or tightness, that echo an earlier trauma.", "The body may react strongly before the mind understands why. The sensations can feel frightening, confusing and very real.", "A person may flinch, freeze, or suddenly rub a body part, without a clear reason at the time.", "These sensations are real reactions, not a sign of being broken. They often ease with trauma-focused care that works at a steady pace.", ""],
  ["SOMATIC MEMORIES", "B", "Body sensations or reactions that seem linked to past experiences, even without clear memories.", "Tension, a sudden shiver or a sinking feeling can come up without a story attached. It can be unsettling when it seems to come from nowhere.", "A person may react to a smell or touch in ways that others find puzzling.", "Body reactions are real signals, but they do not prove a specific past event. A therapist can help you sort out what they mean.", ""],
  ["SOMATIC NARCISSIST", "R", "A person who seeks admiration mainly for physical looks, athletic ability or bodily strength.", "Their sense of worth can depend on praise for appearance or strength. Criticism of their looks or performance can feel like a serious threat.", "Others may notice constant focus on looks or strength, comparisons with others, and little interest in inner qualities.", "Caring about fitness or appearance is common and can be healthy. The pattern matters when self-worth depends on admiration and others' value is judged by looks.", ""],
  ["SOMATIC RELEASE", "K", "A practice of letting stored tension leave the body through shaking, stretching or deep breathing.", "Releasing tension can bring warmth, trembling or a deep sigh. Some people feel lighter afterward, though relief may come in waves.", "A person may stretch slowly, shake out their arms, or take a long exhale after a hard talk.", "Body-based release does not replace treatment for trauma or anxiety. It is one simple tool that can support other care.", ""],
  ["SOMATIC RESOURCING", "K", "Using body sensations of safety, such as warmth, steady feet or slow breath, to feel calmer.", "Noticing a sense of solid ground under the feet can bring a steadier feeling. The effect often builds with repeated, gentle attention.", "A person may press their feet into the floor, lean back into a chair, or rest a hand on the chest.", "This practice is not about forcing calm. It is a gentle way to notice safety in the body, even when the mind is stormy.", ""],
  ["SOMATIC SYMPTOM DISORDER", "D", "A condition with real physical symptoms that cause strong distress, worry and daily disruption.", "The symptoms can be real and tiring, and worry about them can grow over time. Frequent doctor visits may bring little relief.", "A person may describe many physical complaints, seek repeated tests, or struggle with daily activities.", "Symptoms are not invented or faked. Care that addresses both body and mind, with one consistent doctor and a therapist, often works best.", ""],
  ["SOMATIC TENSION", "B", "Muscle tightness or body strain that builds up under stress, often in the neck, jaw or shoulders.", "The body may feel braced, sore or unable to fully relax. Tension can lead to headaches or a sense of constant readiness.", "A person may clench their jaw, hunch their shoulders, or rub their neck during meetings.", "Tension is a normal stress response, but ongoing pain or headaches deserve a doctor's review. Gentle stretching and rest often bring some relief.", ""],
  ["SOMATIC WISDOM HONORING", "U", "Respecting what the body signals about rest, hunger, safety and needs, and responding with care.", "Listening to the body can feel steadying and respectful. It can bring a sense of trust in your own signals.", "A person may take breaks when tired, eat when hungry, or step away when they feel unsafe.", "Body signals are useful information, but they are not always accurate on their own. Combining them with medical care and honest thought works best.", ""],
  ["SOMATIZATION DISORDER", "D", "A long-running pattern of many physical complaints that is linked to stress and emotional distress.", "The physical pain can feel very real and exhausting, and the cycle of symptoms can be draining. Stress can make symptoms louder.", "A person may describe many aches, stomach pains or fatigue, and seek care often across several doctors.", "Symptoms are not made up. Treatment that connects body and emotions, and that builds one steady care team, often helps most.", ""],
  ["SOMNIPHOBE", "P", "A strong fear of sleep, often of nightmares, losing control or what might happen at night.", "Bedtime can bring dread and a racing mind. Even the thought of falling asleep can cause a surge of fear.", "A person may stay up late, nap in short bursts, or seem exhausted and on edge in the evening.", "Fear of sleep is real and can be treated, even when it feels silly. Sleep is a basic need, so help from a doctor or therapist is worth seeking.", ""],
  ["SOMNOPHILE", "F", "A sexual interest in people who are asleep.", "The interest can feel private, confusing and shameful, and it can bring intense urges. Feeling it does not mean a person has acted on it.", "Usually private, with few outward signs, and it may appear only in fantasies or private searches.", "A sleeping person cannot consent, so any sexual contact with them is assault and causes serious harm. Having the interest is not the same as acting on it, and help is available before harm happens.", "A therapist who works with sexual health can help you talk it through. In the US, call or text 988 if you are in crisis. If someone is in danger, call 911."],
  ["SOPHOPHILE", "F", "A love of wisdom, learning and thoughtful ideas, and an eagerness to understand the world.", "Learning can feel exciting and deeply satisfying. Big questions may bring a sense of wonder and calm.", "A person may read widely, discuss ideas, or take classes for the joy of learning.", "A love of learning is a strength, not a sign of arrogance. It stays healthy when it stays open to being wrong.", ""],
  ["SOULFUL", "U", "Feeling deeply, with a sense of meaning, warmth and connection in everyday life.", "Soulful moments can bring tears, awe or a quiet ache that feels meaningful. They can feel grounding and real.", "A person may listen with full attention, speak sincerely, or linger over music, art or nature.", "Being soulful is not the same as being sad or dramatic. It is a way of living with depth and attention.", ""],
  ["SOUND BATH RESET", "K", "A relaxing practice of listening to slow, resonant sounds to ease stress and reset the mind.", "The sound can feel warm and enveloping, with the mind drifting into quiet. Some people feel lighter or sleepy afterward.", "A person may lie down with eyes closed, listen to singing bowls or drones, and take slow breaths.", "Many people find it calming, but research on its health effects is limited. It can support rest, though it is not a cure for anxiety or depression.", ""],
  ["SOVEREIGN AUTONOMY", "U", "Governing your own life with self-direction, making choices that align with your values.", "This can bring confidence and a sense of ownership over daily decisions. It can also feel demanding when others push back.", "A person may decide how to spend time, say no calmly, and take responsibility for their choices.", "Autonomy is not isolation or refusing help. It means choosing your own path and accepting support on your own terms.", ""],
  ["SOVEREIGN INDEPENDENCE", "U", "Standing on your own strengths and judgment, while still allowing healthy connection with others.", "Independence can feel steady and self-assured, with less need for constant approval. Asking for help can still feel like a choice rather than a weakness.", "A person may make decisions calmly, manage daily tasks, and offer help without taking over.", "Independence does not mean doing everything alone. Healthy people depend on one another, and self-reliance includes knowing when to ask.", ""],
  ["SOVEREIGN INNER PEACE", "U", "A deep inner calm you can return to, even when the outside world is noisy.", "This peace can feel rooted and steady, like a center that stays put. Storms may pass over it without carrying you away.", "A person may stay composed in hard moments, speak gently, and rest without guilt.", "Inner peace is not the same as never being upset. It is a steady place that you can come back to after feelings rise.", ""],
  ["SPECIFIC LEARNING DISORDER", "D", "A learning difference that makes one skill, such as reading, writing or math, much harder than expected.", "Effort in that area can feel exhausting, and the gap can bring frustration or embarrassment. Strengths in other areas are still real.", "A child or adult may read slowly, mix up letters, or avoid tasks that involve numbers.", "It is not a sign of low intelligence or lack of effort. Specialized teaching and accommodations can help people learn in their own way.", ""],
  ["SPECIFIC PHOBIA", "P", "A strong, lasting fear of one object or situation, out of proportion to the actual danger.", "Facing the trigger can bring a sudden jolt of fear, a racing heart and a strong urge to escape. Afterward, the fear may feel silly, though it was real.", "A person may avoid the trigger, plan routes around it, or leave a place quickly.", "A phobia is a real fear, not a weakness or a choice. Gradual, structured treatment often helps people feel more in control.", ""],
  ["SPIRITUAL NARCISSIST", "R", "A person who uses spiritual language to seek admiration, status or control over others.", "Others may feel pressure to match the person's piety or to accept their authority. Doubt about the group's honesty can feel unsafe to speak about.", "A person may claim special insight, speak down to people who doubt them, or use faith to win praise.", "Faith and spirituality are personal and valuable. The pattern matters when a spiritual image is used to gain power over others.", ""],
  ["SPIRITUAL TRAUMA", "D", "Deep harm to faith, trust or meaning, often after abuse within a religious or spiritual setting.", "Prayer or ritual may bring painful memories, and trust in community or faith can feel broken. Grief and anger can come together.", "A person may stop attending services, avoid spiritual topics, or become sharply distrustful of leaders.", "Harm done in a spiritual setting is not the victim's fault or a sign of lost worth. Trauma-informed care and a supportive community can help rebuild safety.", ""],
  ["STARTLE REFLEX", "B", "A quick, automatic jolt of the body in response to a sudden sound, light or movement.", "The jump can feel startling, then embarrassing. The heart may race for a moment before it settles.", "A person may flinch, jump, or briefly freeze when a door slams or someone comes up from behind.", "A startle reflex is normal and automatic, not a sign of weakness. A stronger or frequent startle can be linked to stress or trauma and is worth discussing with a doctor.", ""],
  ["STEADFAST", "U", "Loyal, dependable and firm in your values and commitments, even when things get hard.", "Steadfastness can feel grounding and proud. It can also bring tiredness when you keep going for a long time.", "A person may keep promises, show up for friends, and keep working toward goals through setbacks.", "Steadfast does not mean stubborn or unwilling to change. It means staying true to what matters, while still learning.", ""],
  ["STEADY GROUNDED CENTER", "U", "An inner sense of balance you can return to when life feels shaky or chaotic.", "This center can feel like solid ground under the feet and steady breath in the chest. It can make setbacks feel more manageable.", "A person may speak calmly during tense moments, pause before reacting, and return to routines that help.", "A grounded center is not a fixed state you reach once. It is practiced again and again, and it grows with patience.", ""],
  ["STENDHAL SYNDROME", "B", "A reported rush of dizziness, faintness or a racing heart after being overwhelmed by great beauty.", "Standing before a powerful artwork or view can feel almost too intense to hold. The body may respond with lightheadedness or a flood of emotion.", "A person may sit down suddenly, grip a railing, or go quiet while looking at a famous artwork.", "This is a rare, reported reaction, not a formal medical diagnosis. Strong emotion can be felt in the body, and a doctor should check any fainting.", ""],
  ["STEREOTYPED", "T", "Judging people through a fixed, simplified image of the group they belong to.", "This kind of thinking can feel quick and certain, but it can leave people feeling boxed in or misunderstood. It can also shape how we act toward others.", "A person may make sweeping remarks about a group, assume traits from one example, or dismiss individuals by their background.", "Stereotypes are often learned without intent, and everyone holds some. Noticing them is the first step toward seeing people as individuals.", ""],
  ["STEREOTYPIC MOVEMENT DISORDER", "D", "A condition with repeated, purposeless movements such as rocking, hand flapping or head nodding.", "The movements may feel soothing or necessary to the person, even when others find them distracting. Stopping them can feel uncomfortable.", "Others may notice rhythmic rocking, repeated hand motions, or movements that continue for long periods.", "These movements are not a choice or bad behavior. Support from a doctor, occupational therapist, or behavioral specialist can help reduce any harm and improve comfort.", ""],
  ["STIMULANT USE DISORDER", "D", "A pattern of repeated use of stimulant drugs, such as cocaine or methamphetamine, despite harm.", "Cravings can feel strong and hard to resist, and the drug may bring a brief lift followed by a crash. Shame and worry often follow.", "Others may notice changes in sleep, appetite, money, or relationships, plus periods of restlessness or unusual energy.", "This is a treatable condition, not a lack of willpower or a character flaw. Medical care, counseling and support groups can help people reduce use and regain control.", ""],
  ["STOMACH CHURNING/CRAMPS", "B", "Queasy, twisting or cramping feelings in the stomach, often from stress, nerves or illness.", "The stomach may feel tight or knotted, and the sensation can rise and fall with worry. It can make eating feel unappealing.", "A person may hold their stomach, lie down suddenly, or step away from a meal.", "Stomach symptoms are real, and stress strongly affects the gut. Severe pain, vomiting blood, or pain that keeps returning needs prompt medical care.", ""],
  ["STONEWALLER", "R", "A person who shuts down, goes silent or withdraws during conflict instead of talking things through.", "The other person may feel shut out, anxious and unable to resolve the problem. Tension can build until one side erupts.", "Others may notice one-word answers, leaving the room mid-discussion, or ignoring messages after an argument.", "Needing a pause to calm down is healthy when you agree to return. Shutting down for long periods makes conflict harder to resolve.", ""],
  ["STONEWALLING", "C", "Refusing to answer, engage or discuss an issue so the other person cannot resolve it.", "The other person may feel frustrated, powerless and unheard. Problems can sit unresolved, and resentment can grow.", "Others may notice refusals to answer questions, ignoring requests, or saying 'we are done' without any discussion.", "Taking a break to think is different from stonewalling, especially when you name it and agree on a return time. Repeated refusal to engage is a tactic that harms trust.", "The National Domestic Violence Hotline is 1-800-799-7233 in the US. A trusted person, counselor, or advocate can help plan next steps. In the US, call or text 988 if you are in crisis, or call 911 if someone is in danger."],
  ["STRATEGIST", "U", "A person who plans ahead, sees patterns and thinks about the best next step.", "Strategic thinking can feel focused and confident, with a sense of control over the future. It can also feel tiring when every choice is weighed.", "A person may map out steps for a project, anticipate problems, or ask careful questions before deciding.", "Strategic thinking is a strength. It becomes harmful when it treats people only as pieces on a board, which can lead to manipulation.", ""],
  ["STRAW MAN", "T", "A debate error in which someone distorts another's argument into a weaker version and then attacks it.", "Hearing your own view twisted can feel frustrating and unfair. It can make honest discussion harder.", "Others may notice replies that restate a point in exaggerated form and then argue against that version.", "A straw man is a reasoning error, which can happen by accident. Restating the other side fairly before responding keeps the discussion honest.", ""],
  ["STUPOROUS", "B", "A state of near-total stillness and little response, close to a deep, unaware sleep.", "The state can make a person hard to rouse, with little or no response to sound or touch. Speech and thinking may stop almost entirely, which can be frightening for people nearby.", "Others may notice someone who barely moves, does not speak, or responds only to strong pain.", "Stupor is a medical emergency, not a personality trait or a choice. Anyone who cannot be woken or cannot respond needs emergency care right away.", ""],
  ["SUBLIMATION", "K", "Turning strong urges or painful feelings into a constructive outlet, such as art, sport or work.", "Channeling a strong feeling can bring a sense of purpose and some relief. The energy may shift into something you can build on.", "A person may paint after a hard day, run after a tense conflict, or throw themselves into a project.", "Sublimation is not suppression or pretending the feeling is gone. It works best when the feeling is acknowledged and given a healthy outlet.", ""],
  ["SUBMISSOPHILE", "F", "A sexual interest in being submissive or yielding during intimacy.", "The interest can feel private and sometimes confusing, especially if it does not match how you see yourself. It can bring shame or relief.", "Usually private, with few outward signs, and it may appear only in private thoughts or chosen relationships.", "Interest in submission between adults is possible, but consent must be clear, ongoing and freely given, and anyone can stop at any time. Having an interest does not mean acting on it without consent.", "A therapist who works with sexual health can help you talk it through. In the US, call or text 988 if you are in crisis."],
  ["SUBMIT RESPONSE", "B", "A stress reaction in which a person goes quiet and complies to reduce the perceived danger.", "Giving in can feel like a way to stay safe, though it can bring resentment or shame later. The body may feel small and heavy.", "A person may nod, say yes, or stop resisting while looking tense or distant.", "It is a survival reflex, not a choice or a sign of weakness. It can be understood and gently worked on in therapy.", ""],
  ["SUBSTANCE USE AS AVOIDANCE", "V", "Using alcohol or drugs to escape painful feelings, stress or problems that feel too big.", "Relief can come quickly, which makes the habit feel necessary. Afterward, the feelings often return, sometimes stronger, along with guilt.", "Others may notice more drinking or drug use under stress, missed commitments, or avoiding hard talks.", "Using to cope is very common, but it tends to add new problems over time. Support from a doctor or counselor can help you find safer ways to handle pain.", ""],
  ["SUBSTANCE USE DISORDERS", "D", "Ongoing, harmful use of alcohol or drugs that causes distress and trouble in daily life.", "Cravings and the urge to use can feel strong and hard to resist. Guilt, secrecy and fear of stopping can make it harder to ask for help.", "Others may notice missed work or school, changes in money or friendships, or repeated attempts to cut back that do not last.", "This is a treatable medical condition, not a moral failure or a lack of willpower. Many people recover with medical care, counseling and peer support.", ""],
  ["SUPPRESSION", "T", "Pushing thoughts or feelings out of awareness in order to avoid discomfort.", "Holding feelings down can bring short-term calm, but tension often builds in the background. Feelings may leak out later in sudden ways.", "A person may change the subject quickly, appear unusually flat, or stay busy to avoid quiet time.", "Brief suppression can be useful at work or in a crisis. Long-term suppression often adds stress, and naming the feeling tends to lower its intensity.", ""],
  ["SURVEILLANCE", "C", "Monitoring someone's location, messages or activity without their agreement, to control them.", "The person being watched may feel anxious, trapped and unable to have private space. Trust can wear down quickly.", "Others may notice constant checking of phones, demands for passwords, or tracking through shared apps.", "Caring about someone's safety is different from controlling them. Tracking and monitoring without consent is a serious form of control that causes harm.", "The National Domestic Violence Hotline is 1-800-799-7233 in the US. A trusted person, counselor, or advocate can help plan next steps. In the US, call or text 988 if you are in crisis, or call 911 if someone is in danger."],
  ["SURVEILLED", "A", "The sense of being watched or monitored, which can come from real privacy risks or from anxiety.", "Feeling watched can bring unease, a need to hide, and a constant scanning of surroundings. It can be exhausting over time.", "A person may check doors and windows often, avoid phones, or ask whether others are tracking them.", "Some surveillance is real and worth checking, such as a shared device or location app. A trusted person can help review your settings and safety.", ""],
  ["SURVIVOR'S GUILT", "M", "Guilt about having lived through a disaster, loss or harm when others did not survive.", "The guilt can feel heavy and constant, with questions like why you were spared. Joy or relief may feel unwelcome.", "A person may withdraw, speak often about the lost person, or resist enjoying good moments.", "Surviving does not mean you did something wrong. Survivor's guilt is a common response that can ease with grief support and time.", ""],
  ["SWALLOWING REFLEX", "B", "The automatic action that moves food, drink or saliva from the mouth to the throat.", "Most of the time it happens without thought, and it can feel invisible. Trouble with swallowing can feel frightening and tiring.", "A person may cough or choke during meals, take a long time to eat, or drool.", "Swallowing usually works without effort. Ongoing trouble swallowing, frequent choking, or pain when swallowing needs prompt medical review.", ""],
  ["SWEATING", "B", "Moisture released from the skin to cool the body or in response to stress, fear or heat.", "Sweating can feel sticky, hot or embarrassing, especially in public. It often eases as the body cools or the stress passes.", "Others may notice damp palms, a sweaty forehead, or someone wiping their face during tense moments.", "Sweating is a normal way the body cools itself and responds to stress. Heavy night sweats, fever or sweating with chest pain needs medical attention.", ""],
  ["SYLLOGOMANIAC", "F", "A strong, repeated urge to collect and keep things, often until living space becomes cluttered.", "Letting go of items can feel distressing, and the urge to keep them can feel urgent. Clutter may bring shame or worry.", "Others may notice crowded rooms, piles of objects, or a person who struggles to throw things away.", "Collecting can be a hobby that brings joy, and it is not a moral failing. When clutter causes distress or danger, a doctor or therapist can help.", ""],
  ["SYMPHOROPHILIA", "F", "A sexual interest in accidents, disasters or scenes of harm.", "The interest can feel confusing and shameful, especially when it brings up sexual arousal. It is often kept private.", "Usually private, with few outward signs, and it may show only in private searches or fantasies.", "Having this interest is not the same as acting on it. Acting on it would seriously harm real people, and that harm is never acceptable.", "A therapist who works with sexual health can help you talk it through. In the US, call or text 988 if you are in crisis."],
  ["TABOOPHILE", "F", "A pull toward forbidden subjects, rule-breaking or things that people are told to avoid.", "Forbidden topics can feel thrilling, uncomfortable or both. The pull can feel strong when something is off-limits.", "A person may seek out edgy stories, joke about forbidden subjects, or push limits to see what happens.", "Curiosity about taboo topics is common and can be healthy. It becomes a concern when the pull leads to harm for yourself or others.", ""],
  ["TANGENTIAL", "T", "Speech or thinking that drifts off topic and does not return to the main point.", "The speaker may feel their mind racing from one idea to another, and it can be hard to stay on track. Listeners may feel lost.", "Others may notice answers that wander far from the question or stories that never come back to the point.", "Tangents can come from distraction, excitement, anxiety, or a mind that processes many ideas at once. A gentle reminder of the question often helps.", ""],
  ["TAPHOPHOBE", "P", "A strong fear of being buried alive, or of graves, burial and funerals.", "Thinking about burial can bring panic, a racing heart and an urge to escape. Funerals or cemeteries may feel unbearable.", "A person may avoid cemeteries, funerals or movies about burial, or leave a gathering early.", "It is a real phobia that some people share in some form. It responds well to gradual, structured treatment, and it does not mean you are weak.", ""],
  ["TAPPING", "K", "A practice of gently tapping points on the body while naming a worry or a feeling.", "Tapping can feel calming and a little silly at the same time. Some people report relief, though the evidence is still limited.", "A person may drum their fingers when nervous, or tap points on the face or chest while saying a short phrase.", "Tapping is not a proven treatment for mental health conditions. Use it as a gentle, optional tool alongside care from a doctor or therapist.", ""],
  ["TEAR PRODUCTION (LACRIMATION)", "B", "The body's production of tears, called lacrimation, which keeps the eyes moist.", "Tears also rise with strong emotion, irritation or dust in the eye. Crying can bring release for many people, and it is a normal body response.", "A person may tear up during a sad film, rub their eyes, or blink often in dry or windy air.", "Tears are normal, whether they come from sadness, relief or irritation. Constant watery eyes or eye pain should be checked by a doctor.", ""],
  ["TEARS", "M", "Drops of salty fluid from the eyes, which can come from sadness, relief, joy or stress.", "Crying can bring a wave of release, a lump in the throat, or a sense of being overwhelmed. Afterward, many people feel lighter or drained.", "Others may notice someone crying quietly, wiping their eyes, or stepping away to compose themselves.", "Crying is a normal way to release emotion and is not a sign of weakness. It can be a helpful signal that something needs attention.", ""],
  ["TECHNOPHILIA", "F", "A strong love of technology, gadgets and new devices, and of learning how they work.", "New tools can feel exciting and full of possibility. Screens may also pull attention in ways that are hard to control.", "A person may upgrade devices often, read tech news, or spend long periods exploring new apps.", "Liking technology is healthy and common. It becomes a concern when screens crowd out sleep, work, or people you care about.", ""],
  ["TELEPATH", "T", "The belief that you can know what other people are thinking without them saying it.", "This belief can feel very certain in the moment, as if the answer is obvious. Acting on it can bring confusion and strain in relationships.", "A person may answer questions no one asked, or act as if they already know what others are secretly thinking.", "No one can truly read minds, so guesses about others are best checked by asking them directly. Feeling sure is not the same as having proof.", ""],
  ["TEMPERANCE", "U", "Moderation and self-control in food, drink, spending and pleasure, chosen with care.", "Temperance can feel steady and freeing, since you are not ruled by impulses. It may also feel restrictive at first.", "A person may stop after a satisfying amount, plan spending, or choose rest over another late night.", "Temperance is not deprivation or harsh self-denial. It means enjoying life in balanced amounts, with room for pleasure.", ""],
  ["TENDER SELF-COMPASSION", "U", "A gentle, warm kindness toward yourself, especially when you feel hurt or fragile.", "Tender kindness can feel like a soft blanket on a hard day. It may bring tears, and that is part of healing.", "A person may speak softly to themselves, rest without apology, or accept comfort from others.", "Tender self-compassion is not weakness or self-pity. It gives you the steadiness to face hard truths and change.", ""],
  ["TENDER SELF-NURTURING", "K", "Caring for yourself through gentle daily acts, such as warm meals, rest, and kind words.", "Small acts of care can bring warmth and a sense of being looked after. They can build a steady base for harder days.", "A person may take a walk, prepare a simple meal, or set a bedtime they keep.", "Self-nurturing is not indulgence or avoidance of responsibility. It supports your energy and mood so you can meet what matters.", ""],
  ["TERATOPHILE", "F", "A sexual interest in people with visible physical differences or disabilities.", "The interest can feel confusing, shameful or hard to explain, and it may bring strong urges. Feeling it does not mean acting on it.", "Usually private, with few outward signs, and it may show only in private searches or fantasies.", "Disabled and visibly different people are full people, not objects for sexual interest. Treating them that way causes real harm, and acting on it without consent is never acceptable.", "A therapist who works with sexual health can help you talk it through. In the US, call or text 988 if you are in crisis."],
  ["TEST BALLOONING", "C", "Sending a small trial request or boundary test to see how others react before pushing further.", "The person being tested may feel uneasy and unsure whether to comply or push back. Small concessions can start to feel like the new normal.", "Others may notice a small request that becomes bigger when it is accepted, or a limit that is tested again and again.", "Trying something once is not always manipulation, and people can change their minds. The pattern matters when limits are repeatedly tested after each no.", "The National Domestic Violence Hotline is 1-800-799-7233 in the US. A trusted person, counselor, or advocate can help plan next steps. In the US, call or text 988 if you are in crisis, or call 911 if someone is in danger."],
  ["THALASSOPHILIA", "F", "A deep love of the sea, its waves, its sounds and the calm of being near water.", "Being near the sea can feel soothing and expansive, with a sense of freedom. Waves may bring a steady rhythm that eases the mind.", "A person may spend free time by the coast, swim often, or collect shells and photos of the ocean.", "Loving the sea is healthy and common. Water safety still matters, and it is wise to learn about currents and to swim with others.", ""],
  ["THALASSOPHOBIA", "P", "A strong fear of the sea or deep water, including its depth, waves or what lies below.", "Seeing deep water can bring panic, a racing heart or a sense that you are about to be swept away. Even pictures may feel unsettling.", "A person may avoid boats, stay close to the shore, or refuse to enter the water.", "This fear is real and can be treated, even when it feels unreasonable. Gradual, structured support helps many people feel safer near water.", ""],
  ["THANATOMANIAC", "T", "A strong, persistent preoccupation with death, dying or thoughts of ending life.", "Thoughts about death can feel constant and heavy, and they can be hard to switch off. Some people feel a pull toward giving up on life.", "Others may notice talk about death, giving things away, or withdrawing from people and plans.", "These thoughts are painful and serious, but they can change with treatment and support. You do not have to carry them alone.", "If you have thoughts of ending your life, call or text 988 in the US now, and call 911 if you are in danger."]
];
